      "math_academy_id": "e-7951",
      "problem": "Given that the continuous random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-1, 4],$ compute $P(0<   X <  3).$",
      "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14128799/assets/7dd784ec8ea8db9624cd9c945bc8f8e84fd2cbce263f9d19f4506f5417c8f85b.svg)\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-1,4],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14128799/assets/a42eebe866206e69fe64d993746d7e3428a6d1673f27ed92f9a36eed346fc1b6.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<X<3) & =P(0<X<3) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3-0}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
